      minimum: 180,
      model: "MEMORANDUM: Adopting a Unified Digital Certificate Verification Portal. Issue: As enrolment across our English, Qudurat, educational skills, and Quran departments expands rapidly, employers, schools, universities, and parents increasingly contact administrative staff manually to verify printed completion certificates. Manual email verification consumes significant staff hours during peak graduation periods, delays hiring or admission decisions, and exposes unverified paper documents to potential forgery. Options Evaluated: Option A retains manual staff verification, preserving familiar routines yet creating escalating administrative bottlenecks as graduation volume grows each semester. Option B implements an automated database-backed verification portal assigning every graduate a unique identification code and scannable QR verification link. While Option B requires a modest initial development phase, role-based access controls, and strict data-privacy governance, ongoing operating costs remain negligible and verification becomes instantaneous worldwide twenty-four hours a day. Recommendation: It is recommended that the board authorise Option B ahead of next semester's graduations and require that only essential validity data—graduate name, course title, issue date, and active verification status—be displayed publicly. Reallocating saved administrative hours toward live student mentoring will simultaneously strengthen institutional credibility, learner trust, and operational efficiency.",
